        content: `<h2>Childhood in Guangdong, Hawaiian Education, and Medical Career</h2>
<p>Dr. Sun Yat-sen (November 12, 1866 – March 12, 1925), venerated across the Chinese world as the "Father of the Nation" (<em>Guofu</em>), was the premier revolutionary visionary who brought an end to over two thousand years of imperial dynastic rule in China. Born to a modest peasant family in Cuiheng Village, Xiangshan County (now Zhongshan City), Guangdong Province, he was exposed early to stories of the Taiping Rebellion. At age thirteen, he joined his elder brother in Honolulu, Hawaii, studying at Iolani School and Oahu College, where he mastered fluent English and absorbed American constitutional theory, European history, and modern sciences.</p>
<p>Returning to Hong Kong, Sun graduated with distinction from the Hong Kong College of Medicine for Chinese in 1892. While initially practicing medicine in Macau and Guangzhou, Sun quickly concluded that the terminal decay of China’s body politic could not be cured through individual medical treatment: "Treating people is limited; treating the nation is limitless." He abandoned medical practice to dedicate his entire existence to the revolutionary salvation of his homeland.</p>

<h2>The Three Principles of the People and Revolutionary Organizations</h2>
<p>In November 1894 in Honolulu, Sun founded the Revive China Society (<em>Xingzhonghui</em>), raising the clarion call to "expel the Manchus, revive Chinese sovereignty, and establish a unified republic." In August 1905 in Tokyo, Japan, Sun united diverse anti-monarchical societies to establish the Chinese Revolutionary Alliance (<em>Tongmenghui</em>).</p>
<p>During this fertile ideological period, Sun formulated his landmark political philosophy: the <strong>Three Principles of the People</strong> (<em>Sanmin Doctrine</em>):
<ul>
  <li><strong>Nationalism (Minzu):</strong> Overthrowing foreign imperial domination and the corrupt Qing monarchy to restore sovereign dignity and national equality.</li>
  <li><strong>Democracy (Minquan):</strong> Establishing a constitutional republic guaranteeing civil liberties, universal suffrage, and the separation of government powers through his innovative "Five-Power Constitution" (Executive, Legislative, Judicial, Examination, and Control).</li>
  <li><strong>Livelihood (Minsheng):</strong> Ensuring equitable wealth distribution, land equalization, and state regulation of capital to prevent monopolistic exploitation and guarantee food and housing security for all citizens.</li>
</ul></p>

<h2>The 1911 Xinhai Revolution and Founding of the Republic</h2>
<p>Between 1895 and 1911, Sun organized ten daring armed uprisings across southern China, facing severe hardship, exile, and international manhunts, including his famous detention at the Chinese Legation in London in 1896. Undeterred by military setbacks, Sun's relentless international fundraising and political agitation bore historic fruit on October 10, 1911, when the Wuchang Uprising erupted, triggering the nationwide Xinhai Revolution.</p>
<p>Provincial assemblies quickly declared independence from the Qing court. Returning triumphantly from abroad, Sun Yat-sen was elected by representatives of seventeen provinces as the first Provisional President of the Republic of China in Nanjing on January 1, 1912. Although he magnanimously resigned months later to preserve national unity and avoid civil war, Sun's leadership had irrevocably abolished the imperial monarchy that had governed China since Qin Shi Huang in 221 BCE, establishing Asia's first democratic republic.</p>

<h2>Alliance with Global Anti-Imperialism and Enduring Legacy</h2>
<p>In his final years in Guangzhou, confronted by warlord fragmentation and Western imperialist indifference, Dr. Sun boldly reoriented his revolutionary strategy. He instituted the "Three Great Policies": alliance with the Soviet Union, cooperation with the Chinese Communist Party, and assistance to peasants and workers. He reorganized the Kuomintang (KMT) into a disciplined revolutionary party and founded the famous Whampoa Military Academy, appointing Zhou Enlai as political director to train a dedicated modern officer corps.</p>
<p>Traveling to Beijing in late 1924 to negotiate national peace, Sun succumbed to liver cancer on March 12, 1925, at the age of fifty-eight. His famous political testament declared: "The revolution has not yet succeeded; all comrades must strive on!" Sun Yat-sen is uniquely revered today on both sides of the Taiwan Strait and globally as an incorruptible patriot, anti-colonial pioneer, and father of Asian republican democracy whose vision continues to inspire international friendship and sovereign progress.</p>`
